ing World War II, thus fitting squarely within True Crime & Justice while also engaging with historical and political contexts.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"6052bf3c28ef8eb51f9c2b57bb41c027b80b8d3995611cb27b0f2e8e55e867fc","summary":"On February 6, 1945, Robert Brasillach was executed for treason by a French firing squad. He was a writer of some distinction—a prolific novelist and a keen literary critic. He was also a dedicated anti-Semite, an acerbic opponent of French democracy, and editor in chief of the fascist weekly Je Suis Partout, in whose pages he regularly printed wartime denunciations of Jews and resistance activists. Was Brasillach in fact guilty of treason? Was he condemned for his denunciations of the resistance, or singled out as a suspected homosexual? Was it right that he was executed when others, who were directly responsible for the murder of thousands, were set free? Kaplan's meticulous reconstruction of Brasillach's life and trial skirts none of these ethical subtleties: a detective story, a cautionary tale, and a meditation on the disturbing workings of justice and memory, The Collaborator will stand as the definitive account of Brasillach's crime and punishment. A National Book Award Finalist A National Book Critics Circle Award Finalist \"A well-researched and vivid account.\"—John Weightman, New York Review of Books \"A gripping reconstruction of [Brasillach's] trial.\"—The New Yorker \"Readers of this disturbing book will want to find moral touchstones of their own. They're going to need them.","subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"History","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-the-collaborator-the-trial-and-execution-of-robert-brasillach-alice-kaplan"}],"displaySummary":"On February 6, 1945, Robert Brasillach was executed for treason by a French firing squad. He was a writer of some distinction—a prolific novelist and a keen literary critic. He was also a dedicated anti-Semite, an acerbic opponent of French democracy, and editor in chief of the fascist weekly Je Suis Partout, in whose pages he regularly printed wartime denunciations of Jews and resistance activists. Was Brasillach in fact guilty of treason? Was he condemned for his denunciations of the resistance, or singled out as a suspected homosexual? Was it right that he was executed when others, who were directly responsible for the murder of thousands, were set free? Kaplan's meticulous reconstruction of Brasillach's life and trial skirts none of these ethical subtleties: a detective story, a cautionary tale, and a meditation on the disturbing workings of justice and memory, The Collaborator will stand as the definitive account of Brasillach's crime and punishment. A National Book Award Finalist A National Book Critics Circle Award Finalist \"—The New Yorker \"Readers of this disturbing book will want to find moral touchstones of their own. They're going to need them.","primaryTopic":"Regional & Local History","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"True Crime & Justice","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"True Crime & Justice","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"True Crime & Justice","mappedSubject":"True Crime & Justice","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book centers on the trial and execution of Robert Brasillach, exploring themes of justice and ethics surrounding his actions during World War II, thus fitting squarely within True Crime & Justice while also engaging with historical and political contexts.","id":"subject-evidence-book-the-collaborator-the-trial-and-execution-of-robert-brasillach-alice-kaplan-1"},{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"History","mappedSubject":"History","score":3,"confidence":"medium","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-the-collaborator-the-trial-and-execution-of-robert-brasillach-alice-kapl